/*******************************************************************************
    THE SUCCESS LINE DISCRIMINATES, because endCompile is reached by BOTH the
    real path and the not-coded bail, and the flat "succeeded" it used to print
    fired for either. Leaf rules that were never compiled reported "compiling
    tik succeeded" all day. That is an absence-shaped instrument in the sense
    rule H4 forbids: the line could not tell you the thing it named.

    isAction is the discriminator because processCode SETS it on success, and
    it does so by overwriting actionType, which consumes isCoded -- so isCoded
    cannot be read here, it is gone by the time the label is reached. The
    failure path never arrives at all; compile exits on a refused parse.

    The one edge, named rather than hidden: a field that was ALREADY an action
    and is not coded bails and still prints succeeded. walkRules cannot produce
    that, since it gates on actionType being zero before it calls compile.
*******************************************************************************/
compile endCompile active
    if field.isAction   print "compiling " field.tag " succeeded":;
    else                print "compiling " field.tag " SKIPPED -- not coded":;
